{
  "teamId": 117,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Houston Astros arrived with championship expectations and proceeded to meet almost every one of them, finishing 107-55 and leading the American League from wire to wire. They scored 920 runs, clubbed 288 home runs, and posted a team OPS of .847, making them one of the most feared offenses in baseball. Early contributions from unlikely heroes — Robinson Chirinos, Tony Kemp — kept the wins piling up in April, while Alex Bregman's grand slam in May announced that the Astros were operating at full power. The arrival of Yordan Alvarez in late June added a thunderous new dimension to an already stacked lineup, and the team cruised through August and September at a dominant pace despite occasional bullpen hiccups.\n\nThe one persistent flaw in this otherwise stellar season was closer Roberto Osuna, who showed up repeatedly in the most damaging moments. From blown saves against Seattle and Cincinnati in June to a stunning three-run homer surrendered to Oakland's Matt Olson in late July, and most painfully a walk-off home run allowed to Baltimore's Rio Ruiz in August, Osuna was a recurring source of heartbreak despite the team's overall excellence. Yet Houston's depth and offensive firepower almost always absorbed the damage, and the month-by-month record — particularly a blistering 19-6 September — confirmed that these Astros were not merely good, but historically formidable.\n\nThe postseason began efficiently enough, with Houston dispatching the Tampa Bay Rays in the ALDS before staging a memorable ALCS comeback against the New York Yankees. Jose Altuve's two-run walk-off home run in Game 6 of the ALCS off Aroldis Chapman — one of the most electric moments in franchise history — sent the Astros back to the World Series with momentum seemingly on their side. Carlos Correa and Yuli Gurriel had also delivered big blows throughout the playoff run, confirming that this roster was built for October.\n\nBut the World Series against the Washington Nationals became a story of what might have been. Will Harris, one of the most reliable relievers in baseball all season, surrendered two crushing home runs in Games 6 and 7 — including a go-ahead grand slam by Howie Kendrick in the seventh inning of Game 7 that proved to be the fatal blow. The Nationals won the championship, and Houston's 107-win season ended in the cruelest possible way, a heartbreaking conclusion to what had been a magnificent year.",
  "chapters": [
